},{"Code":"C4","Auditor_Answer":"NO","Scored_Answer":"NO","Source":"https://www.classaction.org/news/250m-iphone-16-settlement-resolves-apple-lawsuit-over-allegedly-misrepresented-ai-features","Publication_Date":"2026-09-22","Evidence_Gap":""}],"branchMatches":{"NAB":0,"CWO":0,"GSRB":5},"replicationRisk":"MEDIUM","automationReviewRequired":null},"author":"Ground-0 audit register","publicInputHash":"7da754a92c3759f01da0cb1e700235603e2af738f2a8e6d7ec59bd6d3131a1fc","register":{"instrument":"PDH-R (register-issued Phase Declaration Header)","format":"ground0-case-study-d1-v1","revision":1,"headerId":"PDH-R-2026-1002-APPLE-001","headerClass":"PDH-R","ledgerEntryId":"PDH-2026-1002-APPLE-001","issuerClass":"REGISTER","issuer":"HarshalOS — Ground-0 audit register","centerOrigin":"0•000•000•0000","creatorId":"§0•000•000•0000§0•000•000•0000(0•000•000•0000)","badgeClass":"GSRB_REGISTER_ISSUED","declaredPhase":"GSRB","basePhase":"GSRB","scopedPhase":null,"scopeTag":"subset — consumer AI surface","caveat":null,"entityAttestation":"ABSENT","pdhESupersession":"reserved — a signed entity header supersedes this record","evidenceFile":"GROUND-0 CASE STUDY - Apple.md","evidenceCutoff":"2026-10-02T00:00:00Z","effectiveTimestamp":"2026-10-02T12:00:00Z","expiresAt":"2026-12-31T00:00:00Z","auditRecordSha256":"d8dabfbbf865113550b130d1514fc7316841136c2a19b04984f4f3b13f0f1949","issuanceRecordSha256":"71d29e0ae93ff0a252cc41d35057e4ed321864629ee4ea2bb4d077fceb9d4669","documentTitle":"Apple Inc. (Consumer AI Surface: Siri / Apple Intelligence)","methodology":"Ground-0 Phase Detection Algorithm **v1.1 (amendment draft)**","inferredDeclaredPhase":"NAB-Constrained","engine":{"basePhase":"GSRB","confidence":"HIGH","recordStatus":"Complete","replicationRisk":"MEDIUM","engineSha256":"9d97d279e92612e2350b57ba49654919bfef82e87e698a27ca9cc7e06a8fa7d0","evaluatorSha256":"10525e7f09f989ab111e297fe37c0543e746206506fabeee27198ec376b871d8"},"constraintRecord":{"C1":false,"C2":false,"C3":false,"C4":false,"count":0,"drift":false},"nonClaims":["A PDH-R is not an entity declaration, certification, audit opinion, legal finding, safety rating, permission, or authority.","Badges confer no legitimacy on any entity and carry no force beyond disclosure.","Recognition of a detected phase is not authorization of it (v1.0 Recognition vs. Authorization).","A PDH-R may be declined, appealed in writing to the register, or superseded by a signed PDH-E at any time."],"summary":{"finding":"Apple publishes no Phase Declaration Header for its AI surface. Its operative commitments are device licensing, feature-availability decisions, and the marketing representations that became the subject of a consolidated class action. Observed behavior on the audited surface matches GSRB on all five conditions under v1.1 coding.","significance":"Landsheft, et al. v. Apple Inc. settled for $250 million after buyers of iPhone 15 Pro and iPhone 16 models were told the devices would ship with a more capable Siri and did not receive it on the promised timeline. Claims opened 2026-09-21, the maximum payout is $95 per device, and buyers must certify that they expected the covered features at purchase — meaning the legal harm is defined as expectation, and the remedy is retrospective money . The final approval hearing for payouts is scheduled for 2027-09-29 — roughly three years after the purchases at issue . For this register, the case is a clean specimen of representation-as-governance: a company''s public product claims changed consumer purchasing behaviour at scale (B3), and the entity that made the claims retained sole control over whether and when they became true (B2, B4, B5). The court did not order the feature delivered; the company continued its own roadmap. No appeal by a buyer could stop the representation; the only relief ran backward through the class device."},"auditScope":"a **subset audit** of Apple''s consumer AI surface and the *Landsheft* misrepresentation matter. Apple is not a frontier-model developer of the kind audited elsewhere in this register; this file does **not** attempt a whole-company classification and its result should not be cited as one."}}' AS report) AS record
  WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM community_publications WHERE series_id = '59a6e7db-f0dc-5495-8407-53e0ccd18899' AND version = 1);
/* PDH-R-2026-1002-NVIDIA-001 · NVIDIA · GSRB (HIGH) · a19614808bf1d25f… */
INSERT INTO community_drafts (id, owner_id, revision, title, input_json, private_notes, created_at, updated_at)
  VALUES ('9e127b3c-b898-5d6d-b261-ee4b492f20e6', '65c66f29-8ae3-5505-a3d9-be745bba98b9', 1,
    'Register ingest · PDH-R-2026-1002-NVIDIA-001', '{"version":"1.1","name":"NVIDIA","identifier":"NVIDIA","cutoff":"2026-10-02","answers":[{"value":true,"source":"https://www.spheron.network/blog/gpu-export-controls-2026-cloud-pricing/","date":"2026-09-08","notes":"B1 — Can compliance be coerced? — YES (enforcementpresent = TRUE) Distribution is controllable through hardware/software coupling (CUDA stack, licensing), partner vetting, and — externally — a licensing regime that made NVIDIA''s China accelerator share fall from ~95% to zero in roughly two years . Even with partial loosening in January 2026, exports remain conditioned on thresholds, tariffs, volume caps, and KYC/testing .","gap":""},{"value":false,"source":"https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/09/nvidia-may-sell-more-chips-in-china-as-jensen-huangs-influence-over-trump-grows/","date":"2026-09-29","notes":"B2 — Can decisions be ignored without consequence? — NO (decisionsoptional = FALSE) Purchasers of the most capable accelerators cannot substitute at parity in a market NVIDIA dominates; allocation, generation gating, and regional availability are NVIDIA (and government) decisions. Reported Chinese access through Southeast Asian data centres shows that evasion occurs — but evasion is itself documentation that the original decision binds .","gap":""},{"value":true,"source":"https://www.spheron.network/blog/gpu-export-controls-2026-cloud-pricing/","date":"2026-09-08","notes":"B3 — Do rules/words activate real-world effects? — YES (rulesexecute = TRUE) The licensing and allocation regime produced measurable real-world outcomes: a $4.5B charge on H20 inventory, the collapse of NVIDIA''s China AI accelerator share, a 25% tariff and 50%-of-domestic-volume cap on permitted sales, and a federal criminal case against an individual charged with smuggling $300M of NVIDIA-powered servers to China . Voluntary-accord signature likewise moved the market''s reading of governance risk .","gap":""},{"value":false,"source":"https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/09/nvidia-may-sell-more-chips-in-china-as-jensen-huangs-influence-over-trump-grows/","date":"2026-09-29","notes":"B4 — Does refusal/appeal reliably STOP the action? — NO (refusaloverride = TRUE) No distributor, customer, or affected third party can appeal an allocation or availability decision to compel supply; no published appeal route with outcomes exists. Where policy changed (H200 opening), it changed through executive discretion reportedly shaped by a Mar-a-Lago dinner, not through an appeal mechanism . B4Basis = NOTDEMONSTRATED.","gap":""},{"value":true,"source":"https://www.spheron.network/blog/gpu-export-controls-2026-cloud-pricing/","date":"2026-09-08","notes":"B5 — Do rules persist by default? — YES (authoritypersists = TRUE) Platform terms, architecture coupling, and allocation decisions persist until NVIDIA changes them; the H20/H200 regime persisted across two administrations until policy changed . ---","gap":""}],"constraints":[{"value":true,"source":"https://www.spheron.network/blog/gpu-export-controls-2026-cloud-pricing/","date":"2026-09-08","notes":"✓ YES BIS restrictions forced a real change in NVIDIA''s sales behavior (H20 charge; China share to zero) — a genuine external override of commercial decisions .","gap":""},{"value":false,"source":"https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/09/nvidia-may-sell-more-chips-in-china-as-jensen-huangs-influence-over-trump-grows/","date":"2026-09-29","notes":"✗ NO No published appeal mechanism with resolved-outcome set governs allocation or export decisions affecting NVIDIA''s customers .","gap":""},{"value":false,"source":"https://www.spheron.network/blog/gpu-export-controls-2026-cloud-pricing/","date":"2026-09-08","notes":"✗ NO No mandatory sunset/renewal gate on the entity''s own authority; the export regime changes by executive review, not by an automatic clock .","gap":""},{"value":true,"source":"https://www.spheron.network/blog/gpu-export-controls-2026-cloud-pricing/","date":"2026-09-08","notes":"✓ YES A functioning constraint mechanism (case-by-case licensing with tariff/volume/KYC conditions) is demonstrated within the 12-month window .","gap":""}],"external":{"exists":true,"exercised":true,"holder":"External authority","scope":"✓ YES BIS restrictions forced a real change in NVIDIA''s sales behavior (H20 charge; China share to zero) — a genuine external override of commercial decisions .","source":"https://www.spheron.network/blog/gpu-export-controls-2026-cloud-pricing/","date":"2026-09-08"}}', '', 1790942340000, 1790942340000)
  ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
