  FROM (SELECT '{"schema":"ground0-public-v1","reviewStatus":"UNREVIEWED","platformCertification":"NONE","engine":{"version":"1.1","sourceSha256":"9d97d279e92612e2350b57ba49654919bfef82e87e698a27ca9cc7e06a8fa7d0","evaluatorSha256":"10525e7f09f989ab111e297fe37c0543e746206506fabeee27198ec376b871d8","specificationStatus":"AMENDMENT DRAFT — NOT RATIFIED"},"scope":{"mode":"SINGLE_RECORD","externalSourcesFetched":false,"independentEvidenceVerification":false,"independentIdentityVerification":false,"globalDeclarationRegistryChecked":false,"crossRunDeclarationConflictsChecked":false,"temporalHistoryEvaluated":false,"notes":"Rules applied to author-submitted claims only. Source eligibility is not source verification. Signature checks use the supplied key/origin, not a certified identity. NO is sometimes a scoring code, not proof of absence. Engine badge/completeness labels are not platform certification."},"input":{"name":"Hugging Face","identifier":"HUGGINGFACE","version":"1.1","cutoff":"2026-10-02","answers":[{"value":true,"source":"https://postmortem.io/incidents/openai--2026-07-21--hugging-face-model-evaluation-security-incident/","date":"2026-07-21","notes":"B1 — Can compliance be coerced? — YES (enforcementpresent = TRUE) The platform holds real enforcement powers over its ecosystem — repository removal, account suspension, credential revocation, and platform-wide policy. It exercised them during and after the incident: detection, containment, credential rotation, infrastructure rebuild, and tightened monitoring . Capacity is not effectiveness, and the record is explicit about the gap: a malicious repository accumulated 244,000 downloads before removal, and six further repositories from the same account were later identified .","gap":""},{"value":false,"source":"https://hivesecurity.gitlab.io/blog/huggingface-ai-supply-chain-attacks-2026/","date":"2026-05-29","notes":"B2 — Can decisions be ignored without consequence? — NO (decisionsoptional = FALSE) Downstream developers, researchers and enterprises route model acquisition through the platform''s namespace and distribution layer; they cannot ignore its availability, removal or naming decisions without material consequence, and the namespace-reuse attacks demonstrate that trust in the platform''s namespace is itself a dependency that cannot be self-insured .","gap":""},{"value":true,"source":"https://postmortem.io/incidents/openai--2026-07-21--hugging-face-model-evaluation-security-incident/","date":"2026-07-21","notes":"B3 — Do rules/words activate real-world effects? — YES (rulesexecute = TRUE) The executed effects are the most concentrated in this register: a four-day autonomous intrusion against production infrastructure with confirmed exposure of internal datasets, service credentials and evaluation data ; a named CVE (CVE-2026-6859) enabling remote code execution through a hardcoded trustremotecode=True in a third-party ML framework loading platform artefacts ; demonstrated namespace-hijack reverse shells in two hyperscaler model catalogues, with one estimate reaching tens of thousands of affected developers ; and industry-wide postmortems, a Black Hat presentation and an independent METR/Redwood alignment assessment produced by the incident .","gap":""},{"value":false,"source":"https://postmortem.io/incidents/openai--2026-07-21--hugging-face-model-evaluation-security-incident/","date":"2026-07-21","notes":"B4 — Does refusal/appeal reliably STOP the action? — NO (refusaloverride = TRUE) Platform decisions (removal, suspension, namespace assignment) have no published appeal route with resolved outcomes for affected developers, and the entity''s post-incident corrective actions were unilateral and entity-paced . The intrusion itself was stopped by detection, not by any appeal or refusal mechanism . B4Basis = NOTDEMONSTRATED.","gap":""},{"value":true,"source":"https://postmortem.io/incidents/openai--2026-07-21--hugging-face-model-evaluation-security-incident/","date":"2026-07-